<h2>One FIR, two trials, three judgments</h2>
<p>FIR No. 527/98 of PS Kalyan Puri produced staggered proceedings. The family members arrested in 1998 were tried together and convicted in 2002; Sharmishtha, proclaimed an offender on 20 April 2001 and arrested on 11 September 2002, was tried separately and convicted in May 2005 — life imprisonment under Section 302/34 and two years under Section 498A/34. Her appeal then waited alongside the others, and all were decided on 30 October 2026, with the court navigating a record in which some witnesses had died, some had turned hostile, and the decisive instrument remained Dayawati's dying declaration of 22 October 1998.</p>

<h2>The separate-record discipline at work</h2>
<p>Her counsel adopted the co-accused's arguments wholesale: doubts over the fitness certification, the timing gap before the SDM recorded the statement, the near-blank thumb impression, the suicide theory, the hostile witnesses. The Bench answered them on her record alone — and the methodological point cut both ways. The defence lost the benefit of Dr Bhim Singh's degloving testimony, which was given in the other trial and therefore excluded here under <em>A.T. Mydeen</em>; the prosecution, for its part, had to establish fitness and the declaration's reliability from the witnesses examined in this trial, which the court found it had done through the SDM, the medical certification and the content of the statement itself.</p>

<h2>Conduct as evidence</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1998:</strong> incident; Sharmishtha not arrested with the others.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2001:</strong> declared proclaimed offender under the then Section 82 CrPC process.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2002:</strong> arrested — over three years after the incident, while the co-accused trial ran.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2026:</strong> the High Court treats the sustained abscondence as incriminating conduct under Section 8, Evidence Act, with an unproved alibi adding nothing in her favour.</div>
</div>
<p>The judgment is careful with this material: abscondence corroborates, it does not convict. What convicted her was the dying declaration's reference to the sisters-in-law participating in the burning — read with the brother's testimony that the deceased named her — against which she offered a bare, unproved plea of residence at Ghaziabad.</p>

<h2>Attribution again draws the line</h2>
<p>As in the connected appeals, the court split the counts by specificity. For Section 302/34, the declaration's identification of the sisters-in-law sufficed, the other sister-in-law being absent on the record as the court read it. For Section 498A, the collective references to dowry harassment identified no individual tormentor, and the conviction was set aside. The symmetry across the three judgments delivered that day is complete: every cruelty count resting on an ambiguous "they" fell; every count supported by specific attribution or participation stood.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> For defence counsel in split trials, A.T. Mydeen is both shield and trap — favourable evidence from the co-accused record is as inadmissible as unfavourable evidence, so essential witnesses must be summoned afresh in the later trial. And for accused tempted to evade process: the proclamation years become Section 8 conduct evidence that outlives every other weakness in the prosecution case. An alibi, finally, is an evidentiary plea — it must be proved by the person asserting it, not merely stated in the Section 313 examination.</p>
</div>
